sections.content:
React components are typically written using JSX, a syntax extension that lets you write HTML-like markup directly inside JavaScript code, which is then compiled (by a build tool, handled automatically by React project setups) into regular JavaScript function calls. A basic component looks like this:

function Greeting() {
  return <h1>Hello, world!</h1>;
}

Embedding JavaScript expressions inside JSX uses curly braces:

function Greeting() {
  const name = 'Maya';
  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
}

Key JSX rules that differ from plain HTML: every component must return exactly one root element (wrap multiple elements in a single parent, or use a fragment <>...</>); attributes use camelCase instead of HTML's lowercase (className instead of class, onClick instead of onclick); and self-closing tags must include the closing slash even for elements that don't require it in HTML, like <img />.

