sections.content:
Passing props when using a component, written just like HTML attributes:
<RecipeCard title="Tomato Soup" description="A comforting classic." />

Receiving props inside the component, as a single object parameter (commonly named props):
function RecipeCard(props) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{props.title}</h2>
      <p>{props.description}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

The more common modern convention destructures the props object directly in the function parameter, making each prop available as its own named variable:
function RecipeCard({ title, description }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <p>{description}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

Both versions behave identically; destructuring is simply more concise and avoids repeating props. throughout the component, and is the style you'll see in the overwhelming majority of real React code.

