sections.content:
Basic event handler syntax (note the camelCase event name and the function reference, not a function call, passed in):
<button onClick={handleClick}>Click Me</button>
<button onClick={() => handleClick(someArgument)}>Click Me</button>

Common events: onClick, onChange (fires on input value changes), onSubmit (form submission), onKeyDown, onMouseEnter/onMouseLeave.

A controlled input is a form input whose value is driven entirely by React state, rather than letting the browser's DOM manage its own internal value independently:
function NameInput() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');

  return (
    <input
      type="text"
      value={name}
      onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)}
    />
  );
}

Here, value={name} means the input's displayed value is always exactly whatever name currently is in state, and onChange updates that state on every keystroke — React state and the visible input are kept perfectly, deliberately in sync in both directions.

