sections.content:
1. Ternary expression — for choosing between two pieces of JSX:
{isLoggedIn ? <p>Welcome back!</p> : <p>Please log in.</p>}

2. Logical AND (&&) — for rendering something only if a condition is true, rendering nothing otherwise:
{hasError && <p className="error">Something went wrong.</p>}
(if hasError is false, the expression evaluates to false, and React simply renders nothing for a false value)

3. Early return — for returning entirely different JSX (or nothing) before reaching a component's main return statement, useful when the difference between states is large:
function UserProfile({ user, loading, error }) {
  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error}</p>;
  return <h2>{user.name}</h2>;
}
(this is exactly the pattern already used in lesson 5's UserProfile and PostDetails examples)

sections.code:
function Dashboard({ user, loading, error, notifications }) {
  // Early return: an entirely different UI for the loading state
  if (loading) {
    return <p>Loading dashboard...</p>;
  }

  // Early return: an entirely different UI for the error state
  if (error) {
    return <p className="error">Failed to load dashboard: {error}</p>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Ternary: choosing between two small pieces of JSX */}
      <h1>{user.isAdmin ? 'Admin Dashboard' : 'My Dashboard'}</h1>

      {/* Logical AND: only render this section if there are notifications,
          using a comparison to avoid the '0' pitfall described above */}
      {notifications.length > 0 && (
        <div className="notifications">
          You have {notifications.length} new notifications.
        </div>
      )}

      <p>Welcome, {user.name}!</p>
    </div>
  );
}

