sections.content:
function RecipeList({ recipes }) {
  return (
    <div className="recipe-list">
      {recipes.map((recipe) => (
        <RecipeCard
          key={recipe.id}
          title={recipe.title}
          description={recipe.description}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

Key requirements:
- key must be passed directly on the element returned from .map(), not on some element nested further inside that returned component
- key should be a stable, unique identifier for that specific item — ideally a unique id from your actual data (recipe.id), since this same id will remain attached to the same logical item even if the list's order changes later
- key should almost never be the array's index (recipes.map((recipe, index) => ... key={index})) unless the list is guaranteed to never be reordered, filtered, or have items inserted/removed anywhere except the very end — a constraint real, dynamic lists frequently violate

sections.code:
function TaskList({ tasks, onDelete }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {tasks.map((task) => (
        // CORRECT: task.id is stable and unique, regardless of position
        <li key={task.id}>
          {task.text}
          <button onClick={() => onDelete(task.id)}>Delete</button>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// Example data, where deleting the middle item (id: 2) should NOT
// cause React to confuse task 1 and task 3 with each other:
const tasks = [
  { id: 1, text: 'Buy groceries' },
  { id: 2, text: 'Call dentist' },
  { id: 3, text: 'Write report' }
];

// PROBLEMATIC alternative, using array index as the key instead:
// {tasks.map((task, index) => (
//   <li key={index}>{task.text}</li>
// ))}
// If 'Call dentist' (originally index 1) is deleted, 'Write report'
// shifts from index 2 to index 1. React, matching purely by the index
// key, would think the EXISTING index-1 item simply had its text
// changed, rather than understanding a different logical item now
// occupies that position — usually harmless for simple read-only text,
// but capable of causing serious bugs if that list item contains its
// own internal state or a controlled input tied to a specific task.

